  "subtitle": "A room-scale lamp-and-meter annunciator panel whose ambiguous indicator lights turned a stuck relief valve into a two-hour misdiagnosis — the canonical case study of alarm semantics as interface design.",
  "why_scout_cares": "The TMI-2 control room is the most-cited failure of industrial HCI ever built: hundreds of identical annunciator tiles, no direct indication of a valve's true position, and a color/lamp grammar that told operators the opposite of what was physically happening. It is an artifact that lets the museum talk about alerting, semantic color, alarm flood, and the difference between 'commanded state' and 'actual state' — with photographs that make the interaction legible.",
